  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does the boiler know you turned on a tap?",
      "a": "A flow sensor in the cold-water inlet detects water moving. It signals the control board, which lights the burner straight away.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a combi boiler start when I open the hot tap?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a heat exchanger?",
      "a": "A heat exchanger passes heat from one thing to another without mixing them. In a boiler, hot flame gases flow around thin pipes, heating the water inside.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the flame heat the water?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a boiler have a pipe going outside?",
      "a": "That pipe is the flue. Burning gas makes carbon dioxide, water vapour and sometimes poisonous carbon monoxide, so a fan blows these gases safely outdoors.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the flue for?",
        "Why does my boiler pipe steam in winter?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a condensing boiler?",
      "a": "A condensing boiler cools its exhaust gases until their water vapour turns back into liquid. That releases extra heat into the water, making the boiler much more efficient.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my boiler have a drain pipe?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a combi boiler heat radiators too?",
      "a": "A combi has two jobs. When no tap is open, it heats water that circulates through the radiators; when you open a hot tap, it switches to heating tap water first.",
      "asks": [
        "Does a combi boiler do central heating too?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Combi boilers heat water directly from the mains when a tap is opened, so they need no hot-water cylinder.",
    "Modern condensing boilers can reach about 90 percent efficiency by recovering heat from the water vapour in their exhaust."
  ],
